WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
A3C.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:06.767
A history lesson worth knowing cold: why it mattered, and why we don't use it today.

3
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:10.067
Here's the problem A3C solved.

4
00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:13.933
On-policy methods can't use DQN's replay buffer.

5
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:18.433
But consecutive samples from one environment are hopelessly correlated.

6
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:21.333
The answer: run many environments at once.

7
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:23.733
Here is the design.

8
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:26.533
Each worker has its own environment copy.

9
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:28.900
They compute gradients independently.

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.047
And push them to shared parameters asynchronously, with no locks and no waiting.

11
00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:37.500
Slightly stale gradients turned out to be harmless noise.

12
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.200
It worked remarkably well.

13
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.433
In 2016, sixteen CPU cores matched GPU-trained DQN on Atari.

14
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:48.867
So why did it fade?

15
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:52.667
Synchronous A2C works just as well, and is far simpler.

16
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:57.967
PPO's clipping attacked the stability problem directly, rather than statistically.

17
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:02.833
Its legacy is the parallel rollout pattern every modern library uses.

18
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:05.633
Know the shape, don't build it.

19
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.937
Each worker copies the shared parameters, which may already be stale.

20
00:01:09.937 --> 00:01:10.633
That's fine.

21
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:14.433
It rolls out in its own environment and computes gradients.

22
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:16.767
Then pushes them back without locking.

23
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:19.700
Here's the answer to memorize.

24
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.967
A3C solved on-policy correlation with asynchronous workers instead of a replay buffer.

25
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:31.733
It faded because A2C matched it more simply, and PPO fixed stability at the source.

26
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.533
Its legacy is parallel rollouts, used even by RLlib workers.

27
00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:38.453
Let's recap.

28
00:01:38.453 --> 00:01:41.333
Asynchronous workers replaced the replay buffer.

29
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:43.700
They shared parameters with no locks.

30
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
It faded because A2C is simpler and PPO is more stable.

31
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.611
Its legacy is parallel rollouts.

32
00:01:50.611 --> 00:01:52.200
Next up: actor-critic.
